 },
 {
  "coord": "10:23",
  "verdict": "FAIL",
  "category": "reversal",
  "note": "\"The King\" who loved Mowbray's father is Richard, but a modern reader will take it as Henry IV.",
  "replacement": "MOWBRAY. What did my father lose, in honor, that has to be revived and breathed into me? King Richard loved him. But as matters stood then, he was forced, whether he liked it or not, to banish him. Think of that day, when Henry Bolingbroke and my father sat mounted and fired up in their saddles, their neighing horses daring the spur, their lances at the ready, their visors down, their fiery eyes sparkling through the sights of steel, and the loud trumpet blowing them together. Then, when nothing could have held my father back from Bolingbroke's chest, oh, then the King threw down his baton, and his own life hung on the staff he threw. In throwing it down he threw away himself, and the lives of all those who, by indictment and by the sword, have since come to grief under Bolingbroke."
 },
 {
  "coord": "10:24",
  "verdict": "FAIL",
  "category": "reversal",
  "note": "Referent shifted: the source says nothing that might make others think you enemies, not that makes you think yourselves enemies.",
  "replacement": "WESTMORELAND. You speak, Lord Mowbray, without knowing what you say. The Earl of Hereford was then reputed the most valiant gentleman in England. Who knows on whom fortune would have smiled? But if your father had been the victor there, he'd never have carried it out of Coventry. The whole country cried out in one voice against him. All their prayers and their love were set on Hereford, whom they doted on and blessed and favored, in truth more than they did the King. But this is a mere digression from my purpose. I come here from our princely general to learn your grievances, and to tell you from his Grace that he will give you a hearing. And wherever it shows that your demands are just, you shall have them, with everything set aside that might so much as make anyone think of you as enemies."
 },
